# 1 API Gateway caching - C
Q.append(dict(t="2.1", tp=["Amazon API Gateway", "API caching", "AWS Lambda"],
p="A company runs a product-catalog REST API on Amazon API Gateway with AWS Lambda integrations that query an Amazon Aurora database. More than 90% of requests are GET requests for the same few thousand products, and product data changes only a few times each hour. During marketing campaigns, the backend database becomes overloaded. The company wants to reduce load on the backend and improve response times with the least change to the application. Which solution meets these requirements?",
o=["Configure usage plans with throttling limits on the API stage so that excess GET requests are rejected with HTTP 429 errors during campaigns.",
   "Configure provisioned concurrency on the Lambda functions so that enough execution environments are initialized before each campaign begins.",
   "Enable API Gateway caching on the production stage, include the product ID path parameter in the cache key, and set a TTL of a few minutes.",
   "Place an Application Load Balancer in front of the Lambda functions and enable sticky sessions so that repeat clients reuse the same execution environment."],
c="C",
w=[("Caching at the API layer", "API Gateway REST API stage caching returns cached responses for identical requests without invoking the Lambda integration, so repeated GETs never reach Aurora."),
   ("Correct cache key", "Including the product ID parameter in the cache key ensures each product is cached separately, and a TTL of a few minutes matches data that changes only a few times per hour (the TTL can be 0-3600 seconds)."),
   ("Least change", "Caching is a stage setting; no application code changes are needed.")],
x={"A": "Throttling protects the backend by rejecting legitimate customer requests; it does not improve response times or serve the repeated reads.",
   "B": "Provisioned concurrency reduces Lambda cold starts but every request still queries Aurora, so database load is unchanged.",
   "D": "Lambda functions do not have sticky execution environments behind an ALB, and adding an ALB does not reduce database queries."},
e=[("Read-heavy, rarely changing API responses", "Enable API Gateway (REST API) caching with an appropriate TTL and cache key parameters."),
   ("Throttling vs caching", "Throttling sheds load by rejecting requests; caching absorbs load by answering them from cache.")]))
# 2 Lambda reserved concurrency cap - A
Q.append(dict(t="2.1", tp=["AWS Lambda", "Reserved concurrency", "Amazon EventBridge"],
p="An Amazon EventBridge rule invokes an AWS Lambda function asynchronously for each shipment event. The function calls a third-party carrier API that allows a maximum of 20 concurrent connections from the company; additional connections are rejected and the carrier temporarily blocks the company. During peak periods, the Lambda function scales to several hundred concurrent executions. The company must stay within the carrier's limit without losing events. Which solution meets these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
o=["Set reserved concurrency of 20 on the Lambda function so that excess asynchronous invocations are throttled and retried by Lambda.",
   "Configure provisioned concurrency of 20 on the Lambda function so that only 20 execution environments are available to process events.",
   "Increase the Lambda function timeout to 15 minutes and add exponential backoff in the code when the carrier API rejects a connection.",
   "Increase the memory of the Lambda function so that each invocation completes faster and fewer executions run at the same time."],
c="A",
w=[("Hard concurrency cap", "Reserved concurrency sets the maximum number of concurrent executions for the function, so no more than 20 instances can call the carrier at once."),
   ("No lost events", "For asynchronous invocations, Lambda keeps throttled events in its internal queue and retries them for up to 6 hours, so events are delayed rather than lost."),
   ("Configuration only", "A single function setting enforces the limit without new infrastructure or code.")],
x={"B": "Provisioned concurrency pre-initializes environments but does not cap scaling; the function can still scale beyond 20 using on-demand concurrency.",
   "C": "Backoff in the code does not stop hundreds of concurrent executions from opening connections, so the carrier would still block the company, and long timeouts increase cost.",
   "D": "Faster executions may reduce concurrency somewhat but provide no guarantee that the limit of 20 is never exceeded."},
e=[("\"Downstream system supports only N concurrent calls\"", "Use Lambda reserved concurrency to cap the function at N."),
   ("Reserved vs provisioned concurrency", "Reserved = maximum (and guaranteed) concurrency for a function; provisioned = pre-warmed environments to remove cold starts.")]))
